        <p class="eyebrow">Community science</p>
        <h2>Lizards on the Loose</h2>
        <p>
          Lizards on the Loose is a community science programme run through the
          Fairchild Challenge, the education arm of Fairchild Tropical Botanic
          Garden in Miami. James founded it and continues to lead it.
        </p>
        <p>
          The Fairchild Challenge reaches more than 70,000 K-12 students a year
          across more than 250 south Florida schools, and more than 90% of those
          students are from groups underrepresented in science. Each year, upwards
          of 3,000 middle schoolers from 30 to 60 schools go out and record the
          lizards they find. The programme has produced more than 10,000 verified
          species identification records on iNaturalist, which is to say the
          students are generating usable biodiversity data, not practising on a
          simulation of it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Species identification is ordinarily the barrier that keeps non-experts
          out of this kind of work, and several of the Miami anoles look almost
          identical to a beginner. That is why we built
          <a href="/software/">LizardLens</a>: students get a probability-ranked
          prediction instead of a guess, and correct the model by hand when it is
          wrong. The correction step is the education.
        </p>
      </div>
